DTSTART:20261119T210000
DTEND:20261119T211000
SUMMARY:Day 22 Evening Ledger
DESCRIPTION:Tonight's question: Which plan will be hardest to follow? Rewr
 ite it so it's shorter and easier to say.\n\nTHE LEDGER (10 min)\n1. Lies 
 told today (#): what\, to whom\n2. Lies caught before I spoke (#)\n3. Corr
 ections made (#)\n4. The trigger: who\, where\, how tired I was\, and the 
 fear (failure / rejection / conflict / looking weak)\n5. The story in my h
 ead: "If I tell the truth\, ___ will happen."\n6. What actually happened w
 hen I told the truth\n7. Tomorrow's risk moment\, written as an if-then: "
 If ___\, then I'll say ___."\n8. One line of prayer or thanks
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Day 22 Evening Ledger
TRIGGER:PT0M
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:truth-ledger-69@bethemansystem.com
DTSTAMP:20261028T233650Z
DTSTART:20261120T063000
DTEND:20261120T063500
SUMMARY:Day 23 Morning: Rehearse out loud
DESCRIPTION:Week 4: Replace the Lie. You can't just stop. You have to say 
 something else.\n\nSay each if-then plan out loud three times\, in the car
  or the mirror. Your mouth has practiced the lie for years. Give it practi
 ce with the truth.\n\nBefore you go\, write today's if-then: "If ___ happe
 ns\, then I'll say ___."\n\nWhy it works: Break an automatic habit: notice
  it\, then replace it. Day 5 teaches you your body's alarm. The pause and 
 a prepared true sentence (Week 4) are the replacement response. (Bate\, Ma
 louff\, Thorsteinsson & Bhullar (2011)\, Clinical Psychology Review (meta-
 analysis))
BEGIN:VALARM
ACTION:DISPLAY
DESCRIPTION:Day 23 Morning: Rehearse out loud
TRIGGER:PT0M
END:VALARM
END:VEVENT
